      "content": "Those lingering fragments are the bane of data hygiene, and they are often found in places that are less obvious than the main databases, like old log files or temporary caches. Dealing with these edge cases requires a systematic, forensic approach, which is often more complex than the initial deletion. You need to run comprehensive data discovery sweeps across *all* storage tiers—not just primary databases—to locate these hidden remnants. If the data is truly embedded or unrecoverable without compromising system integrity, you must document *why* it could not be purged, citing the technical limitations, which is your defense against a potential complaint.",
